Certainly, Sir. Below is a compact yet extensible script that enumerates all Windows services, filters those that are stopped, attempts to start them concurrently, logs outcomes to a timestamped CSV, and implements robust error handling:

```powershell
# Define output path with ISO‑8601 timestamp
$log = "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\ServicesRestart_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMddTHHmmss').csv"
"ServiceName,Status,StartTime,Result,ErrorMessage" | Out-File -FilePath $log -Encoding UTF8

# Retrieve stopped services and process in parallel
Get-Service | Where-Object {$_.Status -eq 'Stopped'} | ForEach-Object -Parallel {
    try {
        $svc = $_
        $start = Get-Date
        Start-Service -InputObject $svc -ErrorAction Stop
        $result = 'Started'
        $err = ''
    } catch {
        $result = 'Failed'
        $err = $_.Exception.Message
    } finally {
        $record = "$($svc.Name),Stopped,$($start.ToString('o')),$result,$err"
